{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000002", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Windsor Chair", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 3, "body": "Windsor chairs use turned spindles socketed into a sculpted seat. Step 1: Cut and shape the seat from 2-inch thick pine, 20 inches wide. Step 2: Turn or buy 6 to 8 spindles, about 20 inches long. Step 3: Drill angled holes through the seat for the legs and spindles. Step 4: Drive the legs in with glue, wedging the tenons. Step 5: Fit spindles between seat and top rail. Step 6: Carve the seat saddle for comfort. Step 7: Paint — traditional Windsors are painted, which hides mixed woods.", "capabilities": ["Explain the Windsor chair structure", "Describe seat shaping", "Explain wedged tenons", "Describe spindle fitting", "Explain why Windsors are painted"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Spindle count", "code": "const spindles=7,legs=4;J({joints:spindles+legs});assert(spindles+legs===11,'windsor');", "verified_output": "{\"joints\":11}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Folding Chair", "Build a Farmhouse Table"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000003", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Folding Chair", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 4, "body": "A folding chair needs pivoting front and back frames. Step 1: Build two rectangular frames from 1x3 hardwood — front 18x17 inches, back 18x30. Step 2: Drill pivot holes at the crossing point of each side. Step 3: Join the frames with bolts, washers, and lock nuts — tight enough to hold, loose enough to pivot. Step 4: Add a plywood or slatted seat that hinges to the front frame. Step 5: Add a backrest slat to the rear frame. Step 6: Test the fold and sand all edges round.", "capabilities": ["Describe the two-frame folding design", "Explain pivot-bolt assembly", "Explain lock-nut tension", "Describe the hinged seat", "Explain edge rounding"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Fold geometry", "code": "const open=90,closed=5;const travel=open-closed;J({travel_deg:travel});assert(travel===85,'fold');", "verified_output": "{\"travel_deg\":85}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Farmhouse Table"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000004", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Farmhouse Table", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 5, "body": "A farmhouse table is a thick top on sturdy legs with a lower stretcher. Step 1: Glue up the top from 2x8 boards to 36x72 inches; alternate grain direction. Step 2: Cut four 4x4 legs to 29 inches. Step 3: Build two leg assemblies with aprons, then join them with a long stretcher. Step 4: Attach the top with figure-8 fasteners so it can expand. Step 5: Fill knots, sand to 150 grit, and finish with hardwax oil. Standard dining height is 30 inches including the top.", "capabilities": ["Give farmhouse table dimensions", "Explain alternating grain in glue-ups", "Describe the stretcher structure", "Explain figure-8 fasteners", "Give standard dining height"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Top board count", "code": "const width=36,boardW=7.25;const boards=Math.ceil(width/boardW);J({boards});assert(boards===5,'farmtop');", "verified_output": "{\"boards\":5}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000005", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Trestle Table", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 6, "body": "Trestle tables rest the top on two A-frame trestles. Step 1: Cut the top boards to length — 72 inches for six seats. Step 2: Build two trestles: two splayed legs, a crossbar, and a center post each. Step 3: Join the trestles with a long stretcher beam. Step 4: Peg or bolt all joints — drawbore pegs pull joints tight. Step 5: Set the top on the trestles and fasten from below. The design knocks down flat for moving.", "capabilities": ["Describe trestle table structure", "Explain splayed-leg trestles", "Describe drawbore pegging", "Explain knock-down design", "Give seating length rules"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Seating capacity", "code": "const lenIn=72,perPerson=24;const seats=Math.floor(lenIn/perPerson);J({seats});assert(seats===3,'trestle');", "verified_output": "{\"seats\":3}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000006", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Folding Table", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 7, "body": "A folding table uses hinged legs or a folding frame under a plywood top. Step 1: Cut a 3/4-inch plywood top, 30x48 inches, and edge-band it. Step 2: Build two leg frames from 2x2 lumber. Step 3: Hinge each frame to aprons under the top with sturdy hinges. Step 4: Add folding leg braces that lock open. Step 5: Add a latch to hold the legs closed for storage. Test that it locks open firmly before trusting it with weight.", "capabilities": ["Give folding-table top dimensions", "Describe hinged leg frames", "Explain folding leg braces", "Describe the storage latch", "Explain the lock-open safety test"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Top area", "code": "const w=30,l=48;J({sqft:(w*l/144).toFixed(1)});assert(w*l===1440,'foldtable');", "verified_output": "{\"sqft\":\"10.0\"}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000007", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Wooden Stool", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 1, "body": "A three-legged stool is the simplest seat to build and never wobbles. Step 1: Cut a round or square seat 12 inches across from 1.5-inch stock. Step 2: Cut three legs 18 inches long. Step 3: Drill three angled holes in the seat, splayed outward about 10 degrees. Step 4: Glue and wedge the legs in. Step 5: Add stretchers between the legs for strength. Step 6: Sand and finish. Three legs always find level; four legs need perfect length matching.", "capabilities": ["Explain why three legs never wobble", "Give stool dimensions", "Explain splayed leg angles", "Describe wedged leg joints", "Explain stretcher placement"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Leg splay offset", "code": "const legLen=18,angleDeg=10;const offset=legLen*Math.sin(angleDeg*Math.PI/180);J({offset_in:+offset.toFixed(1)});assert(offset>3&&offset<3.5,'splay');", "verified_output": "{\"offset_in\":3.1}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000008", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Bookshelf", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 8, "body": "A simple bookshelf is two sides, shelves, and a back. Step 1: Cut two sides 72x12 inches from 3/4-inch plywood. Step 2: Cut shelves 1 inch shorter than the inside width. Step 3: Cut dadoes or drill shelf-pin holes in the sides. Step 4: Glue and screw the fixed shelves; use pins for adjustable ones. Step 5: Nail on a 1/4-inch plywood back — it squares the whole case. Step 6: Anchor the finished shelf to the wall with an anti-tip strap.", "capabilities": ["List bookshelf parts", "Explain dadoes vs shelf pins", "Explain why the back panel matters", "Describe fixed vs adjustable shelves", "Explain anti-tip anchoring"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Shelf sag check", "code": "const span=34,thick=0.75;const ratio=span/thick;J({ratio:+ratio.toFixed(1)});assert(ratio<50,'sag');", "verified_output": "{\"ratio\":45.3}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000009", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Workbench", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 9, "body": "A workbench needs mass and a flat top. Step 1: Laminate the top from 2x4s on edge to 3.5 inches thick, 24x60 inches. Step 2: Build four 4x4 legs with heavy stretchers. Step 3: Bolt the leg frames to the top — bolts beat screws for a bench. Step 4: Add a lower shelf for weight and storage. Step 5: Mount a woodworking vise on the front. Step 6: Flatten the top with a hand plane or router sled. Height is typically 34 inches.", "capabilities": ["Give workbench top dimensions", "Explain laminating 2x4s on edge", "Explain bolt vs screw choice", "Describe vise mounting", "Give standard workbench height"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Top weight estimate", "code": "const bf=24*60*3.5/144;const lbs=bf*3.5;J({board_feet:+bf.toFixed(1),lbs:+lbs.toFixed(0)});assert(lbs>100,'benchmass');", "verified_output": "{\"board_feet\":35,\"lbs\":123}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000010", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Toolbox", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 10, "body": "A classic tool tote carries hand tools to the job. Step 1: Cut the bottom 20x8 inches and two ends 8x10 inches with handle cutouts. Step 2: Cut two long sides 20x8 inches. Step 3: Glue and nail the box together. Step 4: Fit a dowel handle through the end cutouts. Step 5: Add a center divider with saw kerfs to hold a handsaw. Step 6: Sand and finish with linseed oil. Keep it light enough to carry loaded.", "capabilities": ["Give toolbox dimensions", "Describe the handle cutouts", "Explain the dowel handle", "Describe the saw-kerf divider", "Explain the loaded-weight rule"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Box volume", "code": "const v=20*8*10;J({cubic_in:v});assert(v===1600,'tote');", "verified_output": "{\"cubic_in\":1600}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000011", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Cutting Board", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 11, "body": "An edge-grain cutting board is a great first glue-up. Step 1: Rip hardwood strips 1.5 inches wide — maple, cherry, or walnut. Step 2: Arrange for grain and color, then glue and clamp flat. Step 3: After 24 hours, plane or sand flat. Step 4: Cut to final size, about 12x18 inches, and round the edges. Step 5: Sand to 220 grit. Step 6: Soak with food-safe mineral oil, then top with beeswax board cream. Never put a wood board in the dishwasher.", "capabilities": ["List suitable cutting-board woods", "Explain strip arrangement", "Describe the glue-up process", "Explain mineral-oil finishing", "Explain the dishwasher rule"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Strip count", "code": "const width=12,stripW=1.5;J({strips:width/stripW});assert(width/stripW===8,'strips');", "verified_output": "{\"strips\":8}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
{"id": "JAH-BUILD-000012", "slug": "tool-builds-public", "title": "Build a Wooden Mallet", "edition": "public", "src": "public-harvest", "built_by": "assistant-built", "record_kind": "tool build record", "ladder_rank": 12, "body": "A joiner's mallet is head plus handle. Step 1: Cut the head from dense hardwood — 4x4x6 inches of beech or maple. Step 2: Cut the handle 12 inches long, shaped to fit the hand. Step 3: Cut a tapered mortise through the head. Step 4: Shape the handle tenon to match and drive it through with glue. Step 5: Wedge the tenon from the top. Step 6: Chamfer all edges so the mallet is comfortable. A wood mallet won't damage chisel handles like steel does.", "capabilities": ["Give mallet head dimensions", "Explain the tapered mortise", "Describe wedging the tenon", "Explain edge chamfering", "Explain why wood mallets protect chisels"], "demo": {"language": "javascript", "title": "Head volume", "code": "const v=4*4*6;J({cubic_in:v});assert(v===96,'mallet');", "verified_output": "{\"cubic_in\":96}", "verified_at": "2026-10-21", "node": "v24.20.0", "run": "node"}, "provenance": {"source_paste": "manons-orders-2026-10-20-database-batch.txt (items 129-131) + field-audit fill", "last_verified": "2026-10-21", "origin": "assistant-built"}, "million_march": {"target": 1000000, "records_now": 12, "status": "march-in-progress"}, "generator": {"hard_cap": 1000000000000000, "ladder": ["simple", "competitive", "mix-and-match", "unmade", "top-notch-or-better", "future-grade", "god-mode"], "self_regulation": true, "stop_when_complete": true}, "ai": {"name": "JAH Tool Builds Librarian", "scope": "archive-aware", "assistant_maker": true}, "links": {"related": ["Build a Ladder-Back Chair", "Build a Windsor Chair", "Build a Folding Chair"], "sibling_edition": "tool-builds-public-signature"}}
